data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 11, the **sync-over-async opt-in for the Cosmos DB provider is removed**. The temporary compatibility shim you configured in .NET 10 no longer exists.

All synchronous LINQ operators — `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()` — and `SaveChanges()` will throw **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime because the Cosmos DB SDK is inherently async-only.

## The fix: switch to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key changes:**
- Return `Task<T>` / `Task` instead of bare types
- Use `ToListAsync()`, `FirstAsync()`, `CountAsync()`, `SaveChangesAsync()`
- Propagate `async/await` up through all callers

The sync-to-async migration needs to go all the way up the call stack to the controller (or middleware). If any caller can't be made async, consider offloading Cosmos queries behind a async-capable service boundary.
